<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Franklin</h1><p class="st-meta">1784 CE – 1788 CE · capital Greeneville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A breakaway state set up by frontier settlers in what is now East Tennessee, which sought admission as the fourteenth state but collapsed within four years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1784 North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> offered its western counties beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachians</a> to Congress. Settlers who felt neglected met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonesborough-tennessee">Jonesborough</a> and declared a separate state, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, with the Revolutionary War officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sevier">John Sevier</a> as governor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeneville-tennessee">Greeneville</a> became the seat of government in 1785.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Franklin adopted a constitution based on North Carolina's, set up courts and counties, and, short of coin, allowed taxes to be paid in goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, whisky and linen. It signed treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> leaders to open land for settlement, provoking frontier warfare. A petition for statehood fell short of the two-thirds of states needed in Congress.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> reasserted control, and rival officials clashed until a skirmish at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sevier">Sevier</a>'s opponent John Tipton's farm in 1788. The government faded away that year. The region was ceded to the federal government in 1790 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, with Sevier its first governor in 1796.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-franklin.jpg" alt="State of Franklin" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Bennett1203 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_of_Franklin_on_US_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1784 CE – 1788 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Greeneville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Sevier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free Republic of Franklin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=state-of-franklin" aria-label="State of Franklin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2594 873 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Franklin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2914 1129L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2914 1129h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2914 1129h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=state-of-franklin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
